  "Race conditions happen in concurrent programs.",
  "Threads share memory within a process.",
  "Make the update indivisible - a lock or an atomic operation - because the failure is a legal interleaving, not a random event.",
  "An increment is read, add, write; two threads can both read the old value and both write the same new one, so one update disappears.",
  "Run your unsynchronised counter 1,000 times with two threads, plot the distribution of final values, then repeat with a lock.",
  "Two threads read the counter as 41 at the same instant. What does each write back?",
  "Write the three machine steps of your one-line increment, then interleave two copies of them on paper.",
  "A race condition is not randomness; it is a set of legal interleavings, some of which are wrong. The classic is read-modify-write: two threads read the same value, both compute the same successor, and one update is lost. Reproducibility is a red herring - the interleaving is rare under test load and common under production load. Fixes remove the interleaving: a mutex around the critical section, an atomic operation, or a design where one owner mutates the state and others send it messages.",
