C("web_rest_idempotency", "Web Application Development", "HTTP methods and idempotency",
  [("Says GET is safe and must not change state", "a GET should not modify anything on the server"),
   ("Says PUT and DELETE are idempotent", "repeating the same PUT or DELETE leaves the same result"),
   ("Says POST is neither safe nor idempotent", "repeating a POST can create a second resource"),
   ("Says the semantics matter to caches, proxies and retries", "clients, proxies and crawlers retry or cache based on those properties")],
  "treating the method as a label chosen by convention, when infrastructure acts on its meaning",
  "We use POST for everything including the search page, since the method is just a naming convention.",
  ("Change the delete link to a POST, because links should not delete things.",
   "the instinct is right but the reason is that GET must be safe, since crawlers and prefetchers follow links"),
  "REST uses HTTP methods.",
  "Return 404 when a resource is missing.",
  "Match the method to the semantics - safe for reads, idempotent for replaceable updates - because browsers, proxies and crawlers act on those guarantees.",
  "Anything that follows links will issue GETs on its own, so a state change behind a GET will eventually be triggered by something that never intended it.",
  "Put a state change behind a GET in a test app, run a link prefetcher over it, and record what happened.",
  "A search engine crawls your admin page and follows every link. Which of your endpoints just ran?",
  "Ask which of your endpoints something else might call without a human deciding to.",
  "HTTP methods carry guarantees other software relies on. GET is safe: crawlers, prefetchers and browsers issue it freely, so state changes behind it get triggered by accident. PUT and DELETE are idempotent, so a client that times out can retry safely. POST is neither, which is why duplicate submissions need an idempotency key. Choosing methods by their semantics is what makes retries, caching and proxies behave rather than corrupt data.",
  "A mobile client retries after a timeout on an order endpoint. Say which method you would expose and what makes the retry safe.",
  [("lecture#web-w6-http", "Week 6: method choice is marked on safety and idempotency, not on style."),
   ("lab#web-w6-brief", "Lab 6 brief: audit your endpoints and correct any state change reachable by GET.")],
  ("lecture#web-w6-2017", "Archived note: the HTTP method is a naming convention."),
  ["safe", "idempotent", "retry", "crawler"]),

